KG42.register("nodes-1f44f81088c2fbffec9f45fd",{"id":"COMP-75186ff50e007d27","code":"CLR005","label":"Utility-maximizing contract selection with feasibility, stability and Pareto-efficiency conditions","definition":"Includes different L5 objectives: an entity selects a contract subset according to C_i(X)=argmax_{X'_i⊆X_i}U_i(X'_i); the market outcome X' satisfies that each selected transaction has at most one contract and the entity feasibility conditions; stability requires individual rationality and the absence of a feasible non-empty blocking contract set; there is also a Pareto efficiency requirement that no participant can be improved without harming others.","layer":"L5","template_code":"BCT","concept_id":"clearing.local","concept_ids":["clearing.local"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["contract_bundle_selection: the available contract set yields the selected-contract correspondence C_i through individual utility maximization.","feasible_contract_outcome: feasibility of the selected contract set X' and of at most one contract per transaction.","allocation_stability: a result predicate of individual rationality and the absence of a blocking contract set.","pareto_efficiency: no improvement exists that does not harm other participants."]},"exclusions":["Day-ahead multi-period contract selection by the DSO, aggregators and prosumers.","The adjusted power of the prosumer is P^f_it−|Y_it|+|Z_it|, and is simultaneously subject to upper and lower bounds on power and cumulative stored energy.","The adjusted total demand of the DSO is P^D_t−|Y_dt|+|Z_dt|; ultimately satisfying the upper and lower bounds is conditional on sufficient resource flexibility.","An aggregator without its own energy activity satisfies |Y_jt|=|Z_jt| in each time period; the extension with its own flexible resources replaces this balance condition with power and energy constraints.","Completely substitutable preferences support negotiation yielding stable outcomes; Pareto efficiency here is an outcome requirement.","When the one-step improvement property is satisfied, first select the minimum number of contracts required to satisfy the constraints, then repeatedly select the maximum utility improvement until there is no gain."],"members":[{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"Iterative price negotiation coordinates agents' agreement on the local allocation of flexibility contracts. Scope: Completed day-ahead flexibility-contract allocation within one distribution network. Condition: Negotiation terminates when no price changes occur during an iteration. Deferred-acceptance agent-to-agent price negotiation can produce a stable local contract allocation when agent preferences are fully substitutable. Scope: Stable-outcome guarantee for the distribution-network flexibility market. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy full substitutability. Each agent selects a subset of its available contracts that maximizes its own utility. Scope: Individual contract-bundle selection by every agent in the local flexibility market. A feasible outcome associates each selected trade with at most one contract. Scope: Feasibility of the selected local flexibility-market outcome. The market should produce a stable allocation: no agent prefers to drop an associated contract, and no group prefers to mutually deviate to a different set of contracts. Scope: Desired outcome of the local flexibility market for the DSO, aggregators and prosumers. The local allocation should be Pareto efficient: no participant can be made better off without making another worse off. Scope: Efficiency requirement for local flexibility-market outcomes. Prosumer contract selection must respect resource power limits and energy-storage limits. Scope: Prosumer contract-bundle selection in the proposed flexible-resource model. At the end of price negotiation, the allocation satisfies the DSO's minimum and maximum total distribution-network demand constraints for each time interval. Scope: Distribution-network demand feasibility of the final local flexibility allocation. Condition: Prosumer resources have sufficient flexibility to satisfy the DSO's demand constraints. Each aggregator's total upstream and downstream contracts must balance separately in every time interval. Scope: Aggregator contract allocations in the baseline flexibility-market model. Condition: Aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy. In this extension, prosumer power and energy constraints replace the aggregator's per-interval upstream-downstream contract-balance constraint. Scope: Optional extension of aggregator contract-bundle feasibility to aggregators with their own flexible energy resources. Condition: Aggregator preferences are extended to represent their own flexible energy resources. Agents first select the minimum number of contracts needed to meet their constraints, then iteratively choose available adjustments providing the greatest utility increase. Selection terminates when none of the assessed adjustments provides further utility. Scope: Individual utility-maximizing contract-selection procedure within local clearing. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy the single improvement property.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Aggregator contract allocations in the baseline flexibility-market model. | Completed day-ahead flexibility-contract allocation within one distribution network. | Desired outcome of the local flexibility market for the DSO, aggregators and prosumers. | Distribution-network demand feasibility of the final local flexibility allocation. | Efficiency requirement for local flexibility-market outcomes. | Feasibility of the selected local flexibility-market outcome. | Individual contract-bundle selection by every agent in the local flexibility market. | Individual utility-maximizing contract-selection procedure within local clearing. | Optional extension of aggregator contract-bundle feasibility to aggregators with their own flexible energy resources. | Prosumer contract-bundle selection in the proposed flexible-resource model. | Stable-outcome guarantee for the distribution-network flexibility market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g01-E27","v141-b-g01-E28","v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e10","v141-b-g02-e13","v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e19","v141-b-g02-e25","v141-b-g02-e27","v141-b-g02-e28"]},"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C007","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C008","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C009","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C010","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C011"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"6b0083479ad6fb44c23a18f62fdd13e1dbd690de876e19c5af280f7060136961","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002","node_id":"clearing.local","function":"clearing.local","unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"The local contract-selection outputs identify an over-demanded trade for price-signal coordination, which increases that trade's seller price by Δp. Scope: Feedback from provisional local contract allocations during each iteration of distribution-network flexibility negotiation. Condition: The trade is selected by its buyer but not its seller, and its buyer price exceeds its seller price. The local contract-selection outputs identify an over-demanded trade for price-signal coordination, which increases that trade's buyer price by Δp. Scope: Feedback from provisional local contract allocations during each iteration of distribution-network flexibility negotiation. Condition: The trade is selected by its buyer but not its seller, and its buyer price does not exceed its seller price. Both buyer and seller prices for every trade are initialized to zero. Scope: Initialization of buyer and seller prices for all potential flexibility trades. Condition: Before iterative negotiation begins. Price negotiation is complete when an iteration produces no price changes. Scope: Termination of the iterative flexibility price-negotiation process. Condition: No price changes occur during an iteration. The DSO continues offering higher prices for unrealized contracts required to meet its demand constraints while those constraints remain unsatisfied. Scope: DSO buyer-side offers for unrealized flexibility contracts needed to meet distribution-network demand constraints. Condition: The DSO's demand constraints remain unsatisfied.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"DSO buyer-side offers for unrealized flexibility contracts needed to meet distribution-network demand constraints. | Feedback from provisional local contract allocations during each iteration of distribution-network flexibility negotiation. | Initialization of buyer and seller prices for all potential flexibility trades. | Termination of the iterative flexibility price-negotiation process.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g02-e21","v141-b-g02-e23","v141-b-g02-e24","v141-b-g02-e25"]},"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","source_rule_sha256":"46899d433b00d37531404790a7463e1f29f20c927f0ad3c8338e3f2685c5d251","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003","node_id":"clearing.local","function":"clearing.local","unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false}],"paper_ids":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e19","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Price Negotiation and Contract Selection","anchor_text":"(6) V. PRICE NEGOTIATION AND CONTRACT SELECTION sit- rgy The fact that the agent preferences are ful","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The fact that the agent preferences are fully substitutable has two further consequences. First, the trading network can reach agreement on a stable outcome using the deferred-acceptance agent-to-agent price-negotiation mechanism from [54]. Second, agents can use a computationally scalable process to select sets of contracts that will maximize their utility, since their preferences satisfy the single improvement property [55].","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The fact that the agent preferences are fully substitutable has two further consequences. First, the trading network can reach agreement on a stable outcome using the deferred-acceptance agent-to-agent price-negotiation mechanism from [54]. Second, agents can use a computationally scalable process to select sets of contracts that will maximize their utility, since their preferences satisfy the single improvement property [55]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Agent Preferences","anchor_text":"contracts associated with each time interval t ∈T , given by, ene ing Yit = {(φ, tφ, pφ) ∈Xi|b(φ) = ","equation_id":"(3)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Each agent i \\in A has a utility function U_i which describes its preferences over sets of contracts. The subset of contracts selected by the agent are given by its choice correspondence, C_i(X) = \\operatorname*{argmax}_{X'_i \\subseteq X_i} U_i(X'_i). \\tag{3}","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Each agent i \\in A has a utility function U_i which describes its preferences over sets of contracts. The subset of contracts selected by the agent are given by its choice correspondence, C_i(X) = \\operatorname*{argmax}_{X'_i \\subseteq X_i} U_i(X'_i). \\tag{3}"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Market Stability and Efficiency","anchor_text":"Uj(Yj ∪Zj) = −∞, otherwise (6) The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle sit- uations whe","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"For a trading network with contracts X, an outcome is a feasible set of contracts X' \\subseteq X, where X' is feasible if each trade \\omega \\in \\tau(X') is associated with at most one contract, and therefore has a unique price.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"For a trading network with contracts X, an outcome is a feasible set of contracts X' \\subseteq X, where X' is feasible if each trade \\omega \\in \\tau(X') is associated with at most one contract, and therefore has a unique price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction; design requirements","anchor_text":"g g q ve r The ﬂexibility market should be opt-in for prosumers, and should account for their incent","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Even when acting autonomously and making individually rational decisions, the DSO, aggregators and prosumers should reach agreement on a stable outcome—a set of individually beneficial transactions no group wishes to mutually deviate from.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Even when acting autonomously and making individually rational decisions, the DSO, aggregators and prosumers should reach agreement on a stable outcome—a set of individually beneficial transactions no group wishes to mutually deviate from."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Market Stability and Efficiency","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 3, MAY 2019 xi- A key aim for market design is to e","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Deﬁnition 1: An outcome X' is stable if it is: 1) Individually Rational: X'_i \\in C_i(X') for all i \\in A. 2) Unblocked: There is no feasible non-empty set B \\subseteq X, such that B \\cap X' = \\emptyset and B_i \\subseteq C_i(B \\cup X'), \\forall i \\in a(B). In other words, an outcome is stable as long as no agent would prefer to drop a contract they are associated with, and no group of agents would prefer to mutually deviate to a different set of contracts.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Deﬁnition 1: An outcome X' is stable if it is: 1) Individually Rational: X'_i \\in C_i(X') for all i \\in A. 2) Unblocked: There is no feasible non-empty set B \\subseteq X, such that B \\cap X' = \\emptyset and B_i \\subseteq C_i(B \\cup X'), \\forall i \\in a(B). In other words, an outcome is stable as long as no agent would prefer to drop a contract they are associated with, and no group of agents would prefer to mutually deviate to a different set of contracts."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction; design requirements","anchor_text":"g g q ve r The ﬂexibility market should be opt-in for prosumers, and should account for their incent","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Market outcomes should satisfy Pareto efficiency, meaning that it should not be possible to make one participant better off without making another worse off.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Market outcomes should satisfy Pareto efficiency, meaning that it should not be possible to make one participant better off without making another worse off."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.B Contract Selection","anchor_text":"contract. Given the single improvement property, the agents can use the m. following process to sele","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 3","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Given the single improvement property, the agents can use the following process to select utility-maximizing contract subsets. A flow chart of the contract selection process for the prosumers is shown in Fig. 3. First, the agents need to ensure their constraints are not violated. The prosumers and DSO have maximum power limits. For each time interval when their maximum power limit is violated, they should select upstream contracts starting with the lowest priced contracts, until the violation is cleared. Similarly, high priced downstream contracts should be selected for each time interval until their minimum power limits are not violated. The prosumers also need to ensure their energy storage constraints are not violated. The prosumers should identify the time intervals when their energy storage limits are violated, and should clear each violation in order from earliest to latest. To clear maximum energy violations, prosumers should select downstream contracts for preceding intervals, ensuring they do not introduce new power or energy constraint violations. The downstream contracts with the highest prices available at each suitable time interval should be compared, to see which gives the greatest increase in marginal utility for the unconstrained utility function. Similarly, the prosumers should select low priced upstream constraints to clear minimum energy level violations. Once the agents have selected the minimum number of contracts necessary to meet their constraints, they should select additional contracts which increase their utility. Iteratively, the agents need to assess the marginal utility provided by each of the lowest priced upstream contracts still available at each time interval, the highest priced downstream contracts still available at each interval and upstream-downstream pairs of these contracts.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Given the single improvement property, the agents can use the following process to select utility-maximizing contract subsets. A flow chart of the contract selection process for the prosumers is shown in Fig. 3. First, the agents need to ensure their constraints are not violated. The prosumers and DSO have maximum power limits. For each time interval when their maximum power limit is violated, they should select upstream contracts starting with the lowest priced contracts, until the violation is cleared. Similarly, high priced downstream contracts should be selected for each time interval until their minimum power limits are not violated. The prosumers also need to ensure their energy storage constraints are not violated. The prosumers should identify the time intervals when their energy storage limits are violated, and should clear each violation in order from earliest to latest. To clear maximum energy violations, prosumers should select downstream contracts for preceding intervals, ensuring they do not introduce new power or energy constraint violations. The downstream contracts with the highest prices available at each suitable time interval should be compared, to see which gives the greatest increase in marginal utility for the unconstrained utility function. Similarly, the prosumers should select low priced upstream constraints to clear minimum energy level violations. Once the agents have selected the minimum number of contracts necessary to meet their constraints, they should select additional contracts which increase their utility. Iteratively, the agents need to assess the marginal utility provided by each of the lowest priced upstream contracts still available at each time interval, the highest priced downstream contracts still available at each interval and upstream-downstream pairs of these contracts."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E22","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Prosumer Preferences","anchor_text":"energy management system is a tool for local demand forecast- ing [49]. In the ﬂexibility market, fo","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In the flexibility market, for upstream contracts \\(Y_i\\) and downstream contracts \\(Z_i\\), prosumer \\(i\\)'s utility function is, \\(U_i(Y_i \\cup Z_i) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\in Z_i}(p_\\omega+\\gamma_i t_\\omega) - \\sum_{(\\phi,t_\\phi,p_\\phi)\\in Y_i}(p_\\phi+\\gamma_i t_\\phi) + \\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal{T}} [ p^s_{it}[P^f_{it}-P^d_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}|]^+ - p^b_{it}[P^d_{it}-P^f_{it}+|Y_{it}|-|Z_{it}|]^+ - c_{2i}(P^f_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}|)^2 ],\\) if \\(\\underline{P}^f_{it} \\le P^f_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}| \\le \\bar{P}^f_{it}, \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(\\underline{E}_{i\\tau} \\le E_{i0}-\\sum_{t=1}^{\\tau}(P^f_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}|)\\le \\bar{E}_{i\\tau}, \\forall \\tau\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(U_i(Y_i\\cup Z_i)=-\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{4}\\)","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"In the flexibility market, for upstream contracts \\(Y_i\\) and downstream contracts \\(Z_i\\), prosumer \\(i\\)'s utility function is, \\(U_i(Y_i \\cup Z_i) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\in Z_i}(p_\\omega+\\gamma_i t_\\omega) - \\sum_{(\\phi,t_\\phi,p_\\phi)\\in Y_i}(p_\\phi+\\gamma_i t_\\phi) + \\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal{T}} [ p^s_{it}[P^f_{it}-P^d_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}|]^+ - p^b_{it}[P^d_{it}-P^f_{it}+|Y_{it}|-|Z_{it}|]^+ - c_{2i}(P^f_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}|)^2 ],\\) if \\(\\underline{P}^f_{it} \\le P^f_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}| \\le \\bar{P}^f_{it}, \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(\\underline{E}_{i\\tau} \\le E_{i0}-\\sum_{t=1}^{\\tau}(P^f_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}|)\\le \\bar{E}_{i\\tau}, \\forall \\tau\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(U_i(Y_i\\cup Z_i)=-\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{4}\\)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Prosumer Preferences","anchor_text":"ON SYSTEM FLEXIBILITY 2131 es than energy obtained in the wholesale market [45]. Therefore, gy it is","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Prosumer \\(i\\)'s flexible energy resource is modeled as a flexible load with energy storage capacity. Let \\(E_{i0}\\) be the initial stored energy and for each time interval \\(t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\), let \\([\\underline{P}^f_{it}, \\bar{P}^f_{it}]\\) be the output power limits and let \\([\\underline{E}_{it}, \\bar{E}_{it}]\\) be the energy storage limits. A range of flexible loads can be modeled using these time varying constraints, for example electric vehicles which must be recharged during certain hours.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Prosumer \\(i\\)'s flexible energy resource is modeled as a flexible load with energy storage capacity. Let \\(E_{i0}\\) be the initial stored energy and for each time interval \\(t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\), let \\([\\underline{P}^f_{it}, \\bar{P}^f_{it}]\\) be the output power limits and let \\([\\underline{E}_{it}, \\bar{E}_{it}]\\) be the energy storage limits. A range of flexible loads can be modeled using these time varying constraints, for example electric vehicles which must be recharged during certain hours."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.B DSO Preferences","anchor_text":"B. DSO Preferences The trading network has a single DSO, d ∈A, which must pr manage minimum and maxi","equation_id":"(5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The trading network has a single DSO, d \\in A, which must manage minimum and maximum limits on the total distribution network demand during each time interval, [\\underline{P}^{D}_{t}, \\bar{P}^{D}_{t}]. The DSO buys flexibility contracts for time intervals when the predicted pre-flexibility market retail demand P^{D}_{t}, t \\in T, violates the maximum demand limits, and can sell contracts during unconstrained intervals. For upstream contracts Y_d and downstream contracts Z_d, the DSO's utility function is given by, U_d(Y_d \\cup Z_d) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega})\\in Z_d} p_{\\omega} - \\sum_{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi})\\in Y_d} p_{\\varphi}, \\text{if } \\underline{P}^{D}_{t} \\le P^{D}_{t}-|Y_{dt}|+|Z_{dt}| \\le \\bar{P}^{D}_{t}, \\forall t\\in T, U_d(Y_d \\cup Z_d) = -\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{5}","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The trading network has a single DSO, d \\in A, which must manage minimum and maximum limits on the total distribution network demand during each time interval, [\\underline{P}^{D}_{t}, \\bar{P}^{D}_{t}]. The DSO buys flexibility contracts for time intervals when the predicted pre-flexibility market retail demand P^{D}_{t}, t \\in T, violates the maximum demand limits, and can sell contracts during unconstrained intervals. For upstream contracts Y_d and downstream contracts Z_d, the DSO's utility function is given by, U_d(Y_d \\cup Z_d) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega})\\in Z_d} p_{\\omega} - \\sum_{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi})\\in Y_d} p_{\\varphi}, \\text{if } \\underline{P}^{D}_{t} \\le P^{D}_{t}-|Y_{dt}|+|Z_{dt}| \\le \\bar{P}^{D}_{t}, \\forall t\\in T, U_d(Y_d \\cup Z_d) = -\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{5}"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e25","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.A Price-Negotiation Mechanism","anchor_text":"ON SYSTEM FLEXIBILITY 2133 The negotiation is complete when no price changes occur during an iterati","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Note that if the prosumer resources have sufficient flexibility to satisfy the DSO's demand constraints, then the constraints will be satisfied at the end of the price-negotiation. The DSO will continue to offer higher prices for unrealized contracts required to meet its demand constraints, as long as the constraints are not satisfied. Eventually, these contracts will become sufficiently valuable, so that they are also selected by the associated prosumers and aggregators.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Note that if the prosumer resources have sufficient flexibility to satisfy the DSO's demand constraints, then the constraints will be satisfied at the end of the price-negotiation. The DSO will continue to offer higher prices for unrealized contracts required to meet its demand constraints, as long as the constraints are not satisfied. Eventually, these contracts will become sufficiently valuable, so that they are also selected by the associated prosumers and aggregators."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E26","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.B DSO Preferences","anchor_text":"B. DSO Preferences The trading network has a single DSO, d ∈A, which must pr manage minimum and maxi","equation_id":"(5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The trading network has a single DSO, \\(d \\in A\\), which must manage minimum and maximum limits on the total distribution network demand during each time interval, \\([\\underline{P}^D_t, \\bar{P}^D_t]\\). The DSO buys flexibility contracts for time intervals when the predicted pre-flexibility market retail demand \\(P^D_t, t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\), violates the maximum demand limits, and can sell contracts during unconstrained intervals. For upstream contracts \\(Y_d\\) and downstream contracts \\(Z_d\\), the DSO's utility function is given by, \\(U_d(Y_d\\cup Z_d) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\in Z_d}p_\\omega - \\sum_{(\\phi,t_\\phi,p_\\phi)\\in Y_d}p_\\phi,\\) if \\(\\underline{P}^D_t \\le P^D_t-|Y_{dt}|+|Z_{dt}| \\le \\bar{P}^D_t, \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(U_d(Y_d\\cup Z_d)=-\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{5}\\)","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The trading network has a single DSO, \\(d \\in A\\), which must manage minimum and maximum limits on the total distribution network demand during each time interval, \\([\\underline{P}^D_t, \\bar{P}^D_t]\\). The DSO buys flexibility contracts for time intervals when the predicted pre-flexibility market retail demand \\(P^D_t, t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\), violates the maximum demand limits, and can sell contracts during unconstrained intervals. For upstream contracts \\(Y_d\\) and downstream contracts \\(Z_d\\), the DSO's utility function is given by, \\(U_d(Y_d\\cup Z_d) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\in Z_d}p_\\omega - \\sum_{(\\phi,t_\\phi,p_\\phi)\\in Y_d}p_\\phi,\\) if \\(\\underline{P}^D_t \\le P^D_t-|Y_{dt}|+|Z_{dt}| \\le \\bar{P}^D_t, \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(U_d(Y_d\\cup Z_d)=-\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{5}\\)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E27","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Aggregator Preferences","anchor_text":"C. Aggregator Preferences Let S ⊆A be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators","equation_id":"(6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Let \\(S \\subseteq A\\) be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators act as intermediaries, buying and selling flexibility contracts from the DSO and prosumers. It is assumed that the aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy. Therefore, their total upstream and downstream contracts for each time interval must be balanced. It is assumed that aggregator \\(j \\in S\\) incurs a transaction cost for each accepted upstream contract, \\(c_{1j} \\ge 0\\). In the flexibility market, for upstream contracts \\(Y_j\\) and downstream contracts \\(Z_j\\), aggregator \\(j\\)'s utility function is given by, \\(U_j(Y_j\\cup Z_j) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\in Z_j}p_\\omega - \\sum_{(\\phi,t_\\phi,p_\\phi)\\in Y_j}p_\\phi - c_{1j}|Y_j|,\\) if \\(|Y_{jt}| = |Z_{jt}|, \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(U_j(Y_j\\cup Z_j)=-\\infty, \\text{otherwise} \\tag{6}\\)","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Let \\(S \\subseteq A\\) be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators act as intermediaries, buying and selling flexibility contracts from the DSO and prosumers. It is assumed that the aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy. Therefore, their total upstream and downstream contracts for each time interval must be balanced. It is assumed that aggregator \\(j \\in S\\) incurs a transaction cost for each accepted upstream contract, \\(c_{1j} \\ge 0\\). In the flexibility market, for upstream contracts \\(Y_j\\) and downstream contracts \\(Z_j\\), aggregator \\(j\\)'s utility function is given by, \\(U_j(Y_j\\cup Z_j) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\in Z_j}p_\\omega - \\sum_{(\\phi,t_\\phi,p_\\phi)\\in Y_j}p_\\phi - c_{1j}|Y_j|,\\) if \\(|Y_{jt}| = |Z_{jt}|, \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(U_j(Y_j\\cup Z_j)=-\\infty, \\text{otherwise} \\tag{6}\\)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Aggregator Preferences","anchor_text":"C. Aggregator Preferences Let S ⊆A be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators","equation_id":"(6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Let S \\subseteq A be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators act as intermediaries, buying and selling flexibility contracts from the DSO and prosumers. It is assumed that the aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy. Therefore, their total upstream and downstream contracts for each time interval must be balanced. It is assumed that aggregator j \\in S incurs a transaction cost for each accepted upstream contract, c_{1j} \\ge 0. In the flexibility market, for upstream contracts Y_j and downstream contracts Z_j, aggregator j's utility function is given by, U_j(Y_j \\cup Z_j) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega})\\in Z_j} p_{\\omega} - \\sum_{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi})\\in Y_j} p_{\\varphi} - c_{1j}|Y_j|, \\text{if } |Y_{jt}|=|Z_{jt}|, \\forall t\\in T, U_j(Y_j \\cup Z_j) = -\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{6}","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Let S \\subseteq A be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators act as intermediaries, buying and selling flexibility contracts from the DSO and prosumers. It is assumed that the aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy. Therefore, their total upstream and downstream contracts for each time interval must be balanced. It is assumed that aggregator j \\in S incurs a transaction cost for each accepted upstream contract, c_{1j} \\ge 0. In the flexibility market, for upstream contracts Y_j and downstream contracts Z_j, aggregator j's utility function is given by, U_j(Y_j \\cup Z_j) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega})\\in Z_j} p_{\\omega} - \\sum_{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi})\\in Y_j} p_{\\varphi} - c_{1j}|Y_j|, \\text{if } |Y_{jt}|=|Z_{jt}|, \\forall t\\in T, U_j(Y_j \\cup Z_j) = -\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{6}"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E28","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Aggregator Preferences; IV. Market Stability and Efficiency","anchor_text":"Uj(Yj ∪Zj) = −∞, otherwise (6) The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle sit- uations whe","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle situations where the aggregators have their own flexible energy resources (e.g. community-scale energy storage systems [50]). In this case, the upstream-downstream contract balance constraint \\(|Y_{jt}| = |Z_{jt}|, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\) would be replaced by the power and energy constraints from the prosumer preferences (4).","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle situations where the aggregators have their own flexible energy resources (e.g. community-scale energy storage systems [50]). In this case, the upstream-downstream contract balance constraint \\(|Y_{jt}| = |Z_{jt}|, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\) would be replaced by the power and energy constraints from the prosumer preferences (4)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e28","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p07-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.B Contract Selection","anchor_text":"on The option or options providing the greatest increase in utility should be selected. When none of","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 3","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The option or options providing the greatest increase in utility should be selected. When none of these options provides further utility, a utility maximizing subset of contracts has been selected.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"The option or options providing the greatest increase in utility should be selected. When none of these options provides further utility, a utility maximizing subset of contracts has been selected."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.A Price-Negotiation Mechanism","anchor_text":"ω ∈Ω. The agents then engage in iterative negotiation with dow their neighbors. Each iteration: no r","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The prices of over-demanded trades are increased. The set of over-demanded trades is given by \\Phi = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega \\mid (\\omega, t_{\\omega}, p_{\\omega}) \\in Y^{*}_{b(\\omega)}, \\omega \\notin Z^{*}_{s(\\omega)}\\}. For each \\omega \\in \\Phi, if p^{b}_{\\omega} > p^{s}_{\\omega}, then p^{s}_{\\omega} is increased by \\Delta p; otherwise, p^{b}_{\\omega} is increased by \\Delta p.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The prices of over-demanded trades are increased. The set of over-demanded trades is given by \\Phi = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega \\mid (\\omega, t_{\\omega}, p_{\\omega}) \\in Y^{*}_{b(\\omega)}, \\omega \\notin Z^{*}_{s(\\omega)}\\}. For each \\omega \\in \\Phi, if p^{b}_{\\omega} > p^{s}_{\\omega}, then p^{s}_{\\omega} is increased by \\Delta p; otherwise, p^{b}_{\\omega} is increased by \\Delta p."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["contract_bundle_selection / feasible_contract_outcome / allocation_stability / pareto_efficiency"],"projection_statuses":["composite"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L5-61394f9a48","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L5","ontological_kind":"composite_rule"},"semantic_definition":{},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","paper_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.local","source_occurrences":[{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"Iterative price negotiation coordinates agents' agreement on the local allocation of flexibility contracts. Scope: Completed day-ahead flexibility-contract allocation within one distribution network. Condition: Negotiation terminates when no price changes occur during an iteration. Deferred-acceptance agent-to-agent price negotiation can produce a stable local contract allocation when agent preferences are fully substitutable. Scope: Stable-outcome guarantee for the distribution-network flexibility market. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy full substitutability. Each agent selects a subset of its available contracts that maximizes its own utility. Scope: Individual contract-bundle selection by every agent in the local flexibility market. A feasible outcome associates each selected trade with at most one contract. Scope: Feasibility of the selected local flexibility-market outcome. The market should produce a stable allocation: no agent prefers to drop an associated contract, and no group prefers to mutually deviate to a different set of contracts. Scope: Desired outcome of the local flexibility market for the DSO, aggregators and prosumers. The local allocation should be Pareto efficient: no participant can be made better off without making another worse off. Scope: Efficiency requirement for local flexibility-market outcomes. Prosumer contract selection must respect resource power limits and energy-storage limits. Scope: Prosumer contract-bundle selection in the proposed flexible-resource model. At the end of price negotiation, the allocation satisfies the DSO's minimum and maximum total distribution-network demand constraints for each time interval. Scope: Distribution-network demand feasibility of the final local flexibility allocation. Condition: Prosumer resources have sufficient flexibility to satisfy the DSO's demand constraints. Each aggregator's total upstream and downstream contracts must balance separately in every time interval. Scope: Aggregator contract allocations in the baseline flexibility-market model. Condition: Aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy. In this extension, prosumer power and energy constraints replace the aggregator's per-interval upstream-downstream contract-balance constraint. Scope: Optional extension of aggregator contract-bundle feasibility to aggregators with their own flexible energy resources. Condition: Aggregator preferences are extended to represent their own flexible energy resources. Agents first select the minimum number of contracts needed to meet their constraints, then iteratively choose available adjustments providing the greatest utility increase. Selection terminates when none of the assessed adjustments provides further utility. Scope: Individual utility-maximizing contract-selection procedure within local clearing. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy the single improvement property.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Aggregator contract allocations in the baseline flexibility-market model. | Completed day-ahead flexibility-contract allocation within one distribution network. | Desired outcome of the local flexibility market for the DSO, aggregators and prosumers. | Distribution-network demand feasibility of the final local flexibility allocation. | Efficiency requirement for local flexibility-market outcomes. | Feasibility of the selected local flexibility-market outcome. | Individual contract-bundle selection by every agent in the local flexibility market. | Individual utility-maximizing contract-selection procedure within local clearing. | Optional extension of aggregator contract-bundle feasibility to aggregators with their own flexible energy resources. | Prosumer contract-bundle selection in the proposed flexible-resource model. | Stable-outcome guarantee for the distribution-network flexibility market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g01-E27","v141-b-g01-E28","v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e10","v141-b-g02-e13","v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e19","v141-b-g02-e25","v141-b-g02-e27","v141-b-g02-e28"]},"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C007","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C008","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C009","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C010","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C011"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","source_rule_sha256":"6b0083479ad6fb44c23a18f62fdd13e1dbd690de876e19c5af280f7060136961"},{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::V::clearing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"The local contract-selection outputs identify an over-demanded trade for price-signal coordination, which increases that trade's seller price by Δp. Scope: Feedback from provisional local contract allocations during each iteration of distribution-network flexibility negotiation. Condition: The trade is selected by its buyer but not its seller, and its buyer price exceeds its seller price. The local contract-selection outputs identify an over-demanded trade for price-signal coordination, which increases that trade's buyer price by Δp. Scope: Feedback from provisional local contract allocations during each iteration of distribution-network flexibility negotiation. Condition: The trade is selected by its buyer but not its seller, and its buyer price does not exceed its seller price. Both buyer and seller prices for every trade are initialized to zero. Scope: Initialization of buyer and seller prices for all potential flexibility trades. Condition: Before iterative negotiation begins. Price negotiation is complete when an iteration produces no price changes. Scope: Termination of the iterative flexibility price-negotiation process. Condition: No price changes occur during an iteration. The DSO continues offering higher prices for unrealized contracts required to meet its demand constraints while those constraints remain unsatisfied. Scope: DSO buyer-side offers for unrealized flexibility contracts needed to meet distribution-network demand constraints. Condition: The DSO's demand constraints remain unsatisfied.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"DSO buyer-side offers for unrealized flexibility contracts needed to meet distribution-network demand constraints. | Feedback from provisional local contract allocations during each iteration of distribution-network flexibility negotiation. | Initialization of buyer and seller prices for all potential flexibility trades. | Termination of the iterative flexibility price-negotiation process.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g02-e21","v141-b-g02-e23","v141-b-g02-e24","v141-b-g02-e25"]},"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","source_rule_sha256":"46899d433b00d37531404790a7463e1f29f20c927f0ad3c8338e3f2685c5d251"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Iterative price negotiation coordinates agents' agreement on the local allocation of flexibility contracts. Scope: Completed day-ahead flexibility-contract allocation within one distribution network. Condition: Negotiation terminates when no price changes occur during an iteration.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E10"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Deferred-acceptance agent-to-agent price negotiation can produce a stable local contract allocation when agent preferences are fully substitutable. Scope: Stable-outcome guarantee for the distribution-network flexibility market. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy full substitutability.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e19"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e19","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Price Negotiation and Contract Selection","anchor_text":"(6) V. PRICE NEGOTIATION AND CONTRACT SELECTION sit- rgy The fact that the agent preferences are ful","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The fact that the agent preferences are fully substitutable has two further consequences. First, the trading network can reach agreement on a stable outcome using the deferred-acceptance agent-to-agent price-negotiation mechanism from [54]. Second, agents can use a computationally scalable process to select sets of contracts that will maximize their utility, since their preferences satisfy the single improvement property [55]."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Each agent selects a subset of its available contracts that maximizes its own utility. Scope: Individual contract-bundle selection by every agent in the local flexibility market.","expression":"C_i(X) = \\operatorname*{argmax}_{X'_i \\subseteq X_i} U_i(X'_i)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x-Xprime-i"],"parameter_refs":["theta-Xi","theta-Ui"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Agent Preferences","anchor_text":"contracts associated with each time interval t ∈T , given by, ene ing Yit = {(φ, tφ, pφ) ∈Xi|b(φ) = ","equation_id":"(3)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Each agent i \\in A has a utility function U_i which describes its preferences over sets of contracts. The subset of contracts selected by the agent are given by its choice correspondence, C_i(X) = \\operatorname*{argmax}_{X'_i \\subseteq X_i} U_i(X'_i). \\tag{3}"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C004","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"A feasible outcome associates each selected trade with at most one contract. Scope: Feasibility of the selected local flexibility-market outcome.","expression":"\\forall \\omega \\in \\tau(X'),\\; |\\{x \\in X' : x = (\\omega, t_\\omega, p_\\omega)\\}| \\le 1","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x-Xprime"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e13"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Market Stability and Efficiency","anchor_text":"Uj(Yj ∪Zj) = −∞, otherwise (6) The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle sit- uations whe","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"For a trading network with contracts X, an outcome is a feasible set of contracts X' \\subseteq X, where X' is feasible if each trade \\omega \\in \\tau(X') is associated with at most one contract, and therefore has a unique price."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C005","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"The market should produce a stable allocation: no agent prefers to drop an associated contract, and no group prefers to mutually deviate to a different set of contracts. Scope: Desired outcome of the local flexibility market for the DSO, aggregators and prosumers.","expression":"X' \\text{ is stable} \\iff \\left( X'_i \\in C_i(X')\\; \\forall i \\in A \\right) \\land \\left( \\nexists B \\subseteq X : B \\neq \\emptyset,\\; B \\text{ feasible},\\; B \\cap X' = \\emptyset,\\; B_i \\subseteq C_i(B \\cup X')\\; \\forall i \\in a(B) \\right)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x-Xprime","x-Xprime-i"],"parameter_refs":["theta-Ui"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g02-e14"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction; design requirements","anchor_text":"g g q ve r The ﬂexibility market should be opt-in for prosumers, and should account for their incent","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Even when acting autonomously and making individually rational decisions, the DSO, aggregators and prosumers should reach agreement on a stable outcome—a set of individually beneficial transactions no group wishes to mutually deviate from."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Market Stability and Efficiency","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 3, MAY 2019 xi- A key aim for market design is to e","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Deﬁnition 1: An outcome X' is stable if it is: 1) Individually Rational: X'_i \\in C_i(X') for all i \\in A. 2) Unblocked: There is no feasible non-empty set B \\subseteq X, such that B \\cap X' = \\emptyset and B_i \\subseteq C_i(B \\cup X'), \\forall i \\in a(B). In other words, an outcome is stable as long as no agent would prefer to drop a contract they are associated with, and no group of agents would prefer to mutually deviate to a different set of contracts."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The local allocation should be Pareto efficient: no participant can be made better off without making another worse off. Scope: Efficiency requirement for local flexibility-market outcomes.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction; design requirements","anchor_text":"g g q ve r The ﬂexibility market should be opt-in for prosumers, and should account for their incent","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Market outcomes should satisfy Pareto efficiency, meaning that it should not be possible to make one participant better off without making another worse off."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Prosumer contract selection must respect resource power limits and energy-storage limits. Scope: Prosumer contract-bundle selection in the proposed flexible-resource model.","expression":"\\underline{P}^{f}_{it} \\le P^{f}_{it} - |Y_{it}| + |Z_{it}| \\le \\bar{P}^{f}_{it},\\; \\forall t \\in T;\\quad \\underline{E}_{i\\tau} \\le E_{i0} - \\sum_{t=1}^{\\tau}\\left(P^{f}_{it} - |Y_{it}| + |Z_{it}|\\right) \\le \\bar{E}_{i\\tau},\\; \\forall \\tau \\in T","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x-Yit","x-Zit"],"parameter_refs":["theta-Pf-it","theta-Ei0","theta-Pf-lower","theta-Pf-upper","theta-E-lower","theta-E-upper"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e27","v141-b-g01-E22","v141-b-g01-E19"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C007","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.B Contract Selection","anchor_text":"contract. Given the single improvement property, the agents can use the m. following process to sele","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 3","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Given the single improvement property, the agents can use the following process to select utility-maximizing contract subsets. A flow chart of the contract selection process for the prosumers is shown in Fig. 3. First, the agents need to ensure their constraints are not violated. The prosumers and DSO have maximum power limits. For each time interval when their maximum power limit is violated, they should select upstream contracts starting with the lowest priced contracts, until the violation is cleared. Similarly, high priced downstream contracts should be selected for each time interval until their minimum power limits are not violated. The prosumers also need to ensure their energy storage constraints are not violated. The prosumers should identify the time intervals when their energy storage limits are violated, and should clear each violation in order from earliest to latest. To clear maximum energy violations, prosumers should select downstream contracts for preceding intervals, ensuring they do not introduce new power or energy constraint violations. The downstream contracts with the highest prices available at each suitable time interval should be compared, to see which gives the greatest increase in marginal utility for the unconstrained utility function. Similarly, the prosumers should select low priced upstream constraints to clear minimum energy level violations. Once the agents have selected the minimum number of contracts necessary to meet their constraints, they should select additional contracts which increase their utility. Iteratively, the agents need to assess the marginal utility provided by each of the lowest priced upstream contracts still available at each time interval, the highest priced downstream contracts still available at each interval and upstream-downstream pairs of these contracts."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E22","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Prosumer Preferences","anchor_text":"energy management system is a tool for local demand forecast- ing [49]. In the ﬂexibility market, fo","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In the flexibility market, for upstream contracts \\(Y_i\\) and downstream contracts \\(Z_i\\), prosumer \\(i\\)'s utility function is, \\(U_i(Y_i \\cup Z_i) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\in Z_i}(p_\\omega+\\gamma_i t_\\omega) - \\sum_{(\\phi,t_\\phi,p_\\phi)\\in Y_i}(p_\\phi+\\gamma_i t_\\phi) + \\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal{T}} [ p^s_{it}[P^f_{it}-P^d_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}|]^+ - p^b_{it}[P^d_{it}-P^f_{it}+|Y_{it}|-|Z_{it}|]^+ - c_{2i}(P^f_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}|)^2 ],\\) if \\(\\underline{P}^f_{it} \\le P^f_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}| \\le \\bar{P}^f_{it}, \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(\\underline{E}_{i\\tau} \\le E_{i0}-\\sum_{t=1}^{\\tau}(P^f_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}|)\\le \\bar{E}_{i\\tau}, \\forall \\tau\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(U_i(Y_i\\cup Z_i)=-\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{4}\\)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Prosumer Preferences","anchor_text":"ON SYSTEM FLEXIBILITY 2131 es than energy obtained in the wholesale market [45]. Therefore, gy it is","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Prosumer \\(i\\)'s flexible energy resource is modeled as a flexible load with energy storage capacity. Let \\(E_{i0}\\) be the initial stored energy and for each time interval \\(t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\), let \\([\\underline{P}^f_{it}, \\bar{P}^f_{it}]\\) be the output power limits and let \\([\\underline{E}_{it}, \\bar{E}_{it}]\\) be the energy storage limits. A range of flexible loads can be modeled using these time varying constraints, for example electric vehicles which must be recharged during certain hours."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C008","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"At the end of price negotiation, the allocation satisfies the DSO's minimum and maximum total distribution-network demand constraints for each time interval. Scope: Distribution-network demand feasibility of the final local flexibility allocation. Condition: Prosumer resources have sufficient flexibility to satisfy the DSO's demand constraints.","expression":"\\underline{P}^{D}_{t} \\le P^{D}_{t} - |Y_{dt}| + |Z_{dt}| \\le \\bar{P}^{D}_{t},\\; \\forall t \\in T","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x-Ydt","x-Zdt"],"parameter_refs":["theta-PD-t","theta-PD-lower","theta-PD-upper"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e10","v141-b-g02-e25","v141-b-g01-E26"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C008","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.B DSO Preferences","anchor_text":"B. DSO Preferences The trading network has a single DSO, d ∈A, which must pr manage minimum and maxi","equation_id":"(5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The trading network has a single DSO, d \\in A, which must manage minimum and maximum limits on the total distribution network demand during each time interval, [\\underline{P}^{D}_{t}, \\bar{P}^{D}_{t}]. The DSO buys flexibility contracts for time intervals when the predicted pre-flexibility market retail demand P^{D}_{t}, t \\in T, violates the maximum demand limits, and can sell contracts during unconstrained intervals. For upstream contracts Y_d and downstream contracts Z_d, the DSO's utility function is given by, U_d(Y_d \\cup Z_d) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega})\\in Z_d} p_{\\omega} - \\sum_{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi})\\in Y_d} p_{\\varphi}, \\text{if } \\underline{P}^{D}_{t} \\le P^{D}_{t}-|Y_{dt}|+|Z_{dt}| \\le \\bar{P}^{D}_{t}, \\forall t\\in T, U_d(Y_d \\cup Z_d) = -\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{5}"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e25","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.A Price-Negotiation Mechanism","anchor_text":"ON SYSTEM FLEXIBILITY 2133 The negotiation is complete when no price changes occur during an iterati","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Note that if the prosumer resources have sufficient flexibility to satisfy the DSO's demand constraints, then the constraints will be satisfied at the end of the price-negotiation. The DSO will continue to offer higher prices for unrealized contracts required to meet its demand constraints, as long as the constraints are not satisfied. Eventually, these contracts will become sufficiently valuable, so that they are also selected by the associated prosumers and aggregators."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E26","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.B DSO Preferences","anchor_text":"B. DSO Preferences The trading network has a single DSO, d ∈A, which must pr manage minimum and maxi","equation_id":"(5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The trading network has a single DSO, \\(d \\in A\\), which must manage minimum and maximum limits on the total distribution network demand during each time interval, \\([\\underline{P}^D_t, \\bar{P}^D_t]\\). The DSO buys flexibility contracts for time intervals when the predicted pre-flexibility market retail demand \\(P^D_t, t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\), violates the maximum demand limits, and can sell contracts during unconstrained intervals. For upstream contracts \\(Y_d\\) and downstream contracts \\(Z_d\\), the DSO's utility function is given by, \\(U_d(Y_d\\cup Z_d) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\in Z_d}p_\\omega - \\sum_{(\\phi,t_\\phi,p_\\phi)\\in Y_d}p_\\phi,\\) if \\(\\underline{P}^D_t \\le P^D_t-|Y_{dt}|+|Z_{dt}| \\le \\bar{P}^D_t, \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(U_d(Y_d\\cup Z_d)=-\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{5}\\)"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C009","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Each aggregator's total upstream and downstream contracts must balance separately in every time interval. Scope: Aggregator contract allocations in the baseline flexibility-market model. Condition: Aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy.","expression":"|Y_{jt}| = |Z_{jt}|,\\; \\forall t \\in T","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x-Yjt","x-Zjt"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E27","v141-b-g02-e11"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C009","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E27","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Aggregator Preferences","anchor_text":"C. Aggregator Preferences Let S ⊆A be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators","equation_id":"(6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Let \\(S \\subseteq A\\) be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators act as intermediaries, buying and selling flexibility contracts from the DSO and prosumers. It is assumed that the aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy. Therefore, their total upstream and downstream contracts for each time interval must be balanced. It is assumed that aggregator \\(j \\in S\\) incurs a transaction cost for each accepted upstream contract, \\(c_{1j} \\ge 0\\). In the flexibility market, for upstream contracts \\(Y_j\\) and downstream contracts \\(Z_j\\), aggregator \\(j\\)'s utility function is given by, \\(U_j(Y_j\\cup Z_j) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\in Z_j}p_\\omega - \\sum_{(\\phi,t_\\phi,p_\\phi)\\in Y_j}p_\\phi - c_{1j}|Y_j|,\\) if \\(|Y_{jt}| = |Z_{jt}|, \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(U_j(Y_j\\cup Z_j)=-\\infty, \\text{otherwise} \\tag{6}\\)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Aggregator Preferences","anchor_text":"C. Aggregator Preferences Let S ⊆A be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators","equation_id":"(6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Let S \\subseteq A be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators act as intermediaries, buying and selling flexibility contracts from the DSO and prosumers. It is assumed that the aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy. Therefore, their total upstream and downstream contracts for each time interval must be balanced. It is assumed that aggregator j \\in S incurs a transaction cost for each accepted upstream contract, c_{1j} \\ge 0. In the flexibility market, for upstream contracts Y_j and downstream contracts Z_j, aggregator j's utility function is given by, U_j(Y_j \\cup Z_j) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega})\\in Z_j} p_{\\omega} - \\sum_{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi})\\in Y_j} p_{\\varphi} - c_{1j}|Y_j|, \\text{if } |Y_{jt}|=|Z_{jt}|, \\forall t\\in T, U_j(Y_j \\cup Z_j) = -\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{6}"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C010","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"In this extension, prosumer power and energy constraints replace the aggregator's per-interval upstream-downstream contract-balance constraint. Scope: Optional extension of aggregator contract-bundle feasibility to aggregators with their own flexible energy resources. Condition: Aggregator preferences are extended to represent their own flexible energy resources.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E28"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C010","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E28","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Aggregator Preferences; IV. Market Stability and Efficiency","anchor_text":"Uj(Yj ∪Zj) = −∞, otherwise (6) The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle sit- uations whe","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle situations where the aggregators have their own flexible energy resources (e.g. community-scale energy storage systems [50]). In this case, the upstream-downstream contract balance constraint \\(|Y_{jt}| = |Z_{jt}|, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\) would be replaced by the power and energy constraints from the prosumer preferences (4)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C011","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Agents first select the minimum number of contracts needed to meet their constraints, then iteratively choose available adjustments providing the greatest utility increase. Selection terminates when none of the assessed adjustments provides further utility. Scope: Individual utility-maximizing contract-selection procedure within local clearing. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy the single improvement property.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e27","v141-b-g02-e28"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C011","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.B Contract Selection","anchor_text":"contract. Given the single improvement property, the agents can use the m. following process to sele","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 3","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Given the single improvement property, the agents can use the following process to select utility-maximizing contract subsets. A flow chart of the contract selection process for the prosumers is shown in Fig. 3. First, the agents need to ensure their constraints are not violated. The prosumers and DSO have maximum power limits. For each time interval when their maximum power limit is violated, they should select upstream contracts starting with the lowest priced contracts, until the violation is cleared. Similarly, high priced downstream contracts should be selected for each time interval until their minimum power limits are not violated. The prosumers also need to ensure their energy storage constraints are not violated. The prosumers should identify the time intervals when their energy storage limits are violated, and should clear each violation in order from earliest to latest. To clear maximum energy violations, prosumers should select downstream contracts for preceding intervals, ensuring they do not introduce new power or energy constraint violations. The downstream contracts with the highest prices available at each suitable time interval should be compared, to see which gives the greatest increase in marginal utility for the unconstrained utility function. Similarly, the prosumers should select low priced upstream constraints to clear minimum energy level violations. Once the agents have selected the minimum number of contracts necessary to meet their constraints, they should select additional contracts which increase their utility. Iteratively, the agents need to assess the marginal utility provided by each of the lowest priced upstream contracts still available at each time interval, the highest priced downstream contracts still available at each interval and upstream-downstream pairs of these contracts."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e28","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p07-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.B Contract Selection","anchor_text":"on The option or options providing the greatest increase in utility should be selected. When none of","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 3","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The option or options providing the greatest increase in utility should be selected. When none of these options provides further utility, a utility maximizing subset of contracts has been selected."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The local contract-selection outputs identify an over-demanded trade for price-signal coordination, which increases that trade's seller price by Δp. Scope: Feedback from provisional local contract allocations during each iteration of distribution-network flexibility negotiation. Condition: The trade is selected by its buyer but not its seller, and its buyer price exceeds its seller price.","expression":"Φ^{(k)} = {ω ∈ Ω | (ω, t_ω, p_ω) ∈ Y^{*,k}_{b(ω)} and ω ∉ Z^{*,k}_{s(ω)}}; ∀ω ∈ Φ^{(k)}, if p^{b,(k)}_{ω} > p^{s,(k)}_{ω} then p^{s,(k+1)}_{ω} = p^{s,(k)}_{ω} + Δp","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-X001","v141-c-R003-X002","v141-c-R003-X003","v141-c-R003-X004","v141-c-R003-X005","v141-c-R003-X006"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-T001","v141-c-R003-T002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g02-e23"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.A Price-Negotiation Mechanism","anchor_text":"ω ∈Ω. The agents then engage in iterative negotiation with dow their neighbors. Each iteration: no r","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The prices of over-demanded trades are increased. The set of over-demanded trades is given by \\Phi = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega \\mid (\\omega, t_{\\omega}, p_{\\omega}) \\in Y^{*}_{b(\\omega)}, \\omega \\notin Z^{*}_{s(\\omega)}\\}. For each \\omega \\in \\Phi, if p^{b}_{\\omega} > p^{s}_{\\omega}, then p^{s}_{\\omega} is increased by \\Delta p; otherwise, p^{b}_{\\omega} is increased by \\Delta p."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The local contract-selection outputs identify an over-demanded trade for price-signal coordination, which increases that trade's buyer price by Δp. Scope: Feedback from provisional local contract allocations during each iteration of distribution-network flexibility negotiation. Condition: The trade is selected by its buyer but not its seller, and its buyer price does not exceed its seller price.","expression":"Φ^{(k)} = {ω ∈ Ω | (ω, t_ω, p_ω) ∈ Y^{*,k}_{b(ω)} and ω ∉ Z^{*,k}_{s(ω)}}; ∀ω ∈ Φ^{(k)}, if p^{b,(k)}_{ω} ≤ p^{s,(k)}_{ω} then p^{b,(k+1)}_{ω} = p^{b,(k)}_{ω} + Δp","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-X001","v141-c-R003-X002","v141-c-R003-X003","v141-c-R003-X004","v141-c-R003-X005","v141-c-R003-X006"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-T001","v141-c-R003-T002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g02-e23"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e23","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.A Price-Negotiation Mechanism","anchor_text":"ω ∈Ω. The agents then engage in iterative negotiation with dow their neighbors. Each iteration: no r","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The prices of over-demanded trades are increased. The set of over-demanded trades is given by \\Phi = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega \\mid (\\omega, t_{\\omega}, p_{\\omega}) \\in Y^{*}_{b(\\omega)}, \\omega \\notin Z^{*}_{s(\\omega)}\\}. For each \\omega \\in \\Phi, if p^{b}_{\\omega} > p^{s}_{\\omega}, then p^{s}_{\\omega} is increased by \\Delta p; otherwise, p^{b}_{\\omega} is increased by \\Delta p."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"x-Xprime","symbol":"X'","meaning":"Feasible/selected set of contracts constituting the local market outcome.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"X' \\subseteq X, where X is the set of contracts between the agents; feasibility requires each trade in \\tau(X') to be associated with at most one contract.","unit":null,"indices":["trading_network"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e13","v141-b-g02-e14"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::x::x-Xprime"},{"variable_id":"x-Xprime-i","symbol":"X'_i","meaning":"Agent i's selected subset of its available contracts.","kind":"operational","domain":"X'_i \\subseteq X_i","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e14"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::x::x-Xprime-i"},{"variable_id":"x-Yit","symbol":"Y_{it}","meaning":"Agent i's upstream contracts for time interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Y_{it} = \\{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi}) \\in X_i \\mid b(\\varphi)=i, t_{\\varphi}=t\\}","unit":null,"indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g02-e03"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::x::x-Yit"},{"variable_id":"x-Zit","symbol":"Z_{it}","meaning":"Agent i's downstream contracts for time interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Z_{it} = \\{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega}) \\in X_i \\mid s(\\omega)=i, t_{\\omega}=t\\}","unit":null,"indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g02-e03"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::x::x-Zit"},{"variable_id":"x-Ydt","symbol":"Y_{dt}","meaning":"DSO d's upstream contracts for time interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Y_{dt} = \\{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi}) \\in X_d \\mid b(\\varphi)=d, t_{\\varphi}=t\\}","unit":null,"indices":["d","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::x::x-Ydt"},{"variable_id":"x-Zdt","symbol":"Z_{dt}","meaning":"DSO d's downstream contracts for time interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Z_{dt} = \\{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega}) \\in X_d \\mid s(\\omega)=d, t_{\\omega}=t\\}","unit":null,"indices":["d","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::x::x-Zdt"},{"variable_id":"x-Yjt","symbol":"Y_{jt}","meaning":"Aggregator j's upstream contracts for time interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Y_{jt} = \\{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi}) \\in X_j \\mid b(\\varphi)=j, t_{\\varphi}=t\\}","unit":null,"indices":["j","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E27","v141-b-g02-e11"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::x::x-Yjt"},{"variable_id":"x-Zjt","symbol":"Z_{jt}","meaning":"Aggregator j's downstream contracts for time interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Z_{jt} = \\{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega}) \\in X_j \\mid s(\\omega)=j, t_{\\omega}=t\\}","unit":null,"indices":["j","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E27","v141-b-g02-e11"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::x::x-Zjt"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X001","symbol":"p^{b,(k)}_{ω}","meaning":"Buyer price assigned to trade ω in negotiation iteration k.","kind":"operational","domain":"Price values for trades ω ∈ Ω; source initializes to 0 and does not state explicit bounds.","unit":null,"indices":["ω ∈ Ω","iteration k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e21","v141-b-g02-e23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-X001"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X002","symbol":"p^{s,(k)}_{ω}","meaning":"Seller price assigned to trade ω in negotiation iteration k.","kind":"operational","domain":"Price values for trades ω ∈ Ω; source initializes to 0 and does not state explicit bounds.","unit":null,"indices":["ω ∈ Ω","iteration k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e21","v141-b-g02-e23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-X002"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X003","symbol":"p_{ω}","meaning":"Price specified by a contract for trade ω, used in the selected-contract tuple representation.","kind":"operational","domain":"Contract price value for trade ω ∈ Ω.","unit":null,"indices":["ω ∈ Ω"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e21","v141-b-g02-e23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-X003"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X004","symbol":"Φ^{(k)}","meaning":"Set of over-demanded trades in iteration k: trades selected by their buyer but not their seller.","kind":"operational","domain":"Subsets of Ω, i.e. 2^{Ω}.","unit":null,"indices":["iteration k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-X004"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X005","symbol":"Y^{*,k}_{i}","meaning":"Agent i's selected upstream contracts in iteration k.","kind":"operational","domain":"Subsets of agent i's available upstream contracts Y_i.","unit":null,"indices":["i ∈ A","iteration k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e22","v141-b-g02-e23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-X005"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X006","symbol":"Z^{*,k}_{i}","meaning":"Agent i's selected downstream contracts in iteration k.","kind":"operational","domain":"Subsets of agent i's available downstream contracts Z_i.","unit":null,"indices":["i ∈ A","iteration k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e22","v141-b-g02-e23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-X006"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"theta-Xi","symbol":"X_i","meaning":"Agent i's available contracts, from which the choice correspondence selects a subset.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Agent-specific set of available contracts X_i"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-g02-e04"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::theta::theta-Xi"},{"parameter_id":"theta-Ui","symbol":"U_i","meaning":"Agent i's utility function over sets of contracts; source equations (4)-(6) define it for prosumers, the DSO and aggregators.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Agent-specific utility function defined by source equations (4)-(6) for the relevant agent type"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g01-E22","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g01-E27"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::theta::theta-Ui"},{"parameter_id":"theta-Pf-it","symbol":"P^{f}_{it}","meaning":"Prosumer i's pre-market planned retail flexible demand for interval t, used as a fixed input in the flexibility market.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined pre-market planned flexible demand for prosumer i at interval t"},"unit":"per-contract units","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::theta::theta-Pf-it"},{"parameter_id":"theta-Ei0","symbol":"E_{i0}","meaning":"Prosumer i's initial stored energy.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined initial stored energy for prosumer i"},"unit":"per-contract units","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::theta::theta-Ei0"},{"parameter_id":"theta-Pf-lower","symbol":"\\underline{P}^{f}_{it}","meaning":"Lower output-power limit for prosumer i's flexible resource at interval t.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined lower flexible power limit"},"unit":"per-contract units","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E19"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::theta::theta-Pf-lower"},{"parameter_id":"theta-Pf-upper","symbol":"\\bar{P}^{f}_{it}","meaning":"Upper output-power limit for prosumer i's flexible resource at interval t.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined upper flexible power limit"},"unit":"per-contract units","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E19"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::theta::theta-Pf-upper"},{"parameter_id":"theta-E-lower","symbol":"\\underline{E}_{i\\tau}","meaning":"Lower energy-storage limit for prosumer i at interval \\tau.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined lower energy storage limit"},"unit":"per-contract units","indices":["i","\\tau"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E22"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::theta::theta-E-lower"},{"parameter_id":"theta-E-upper","symbol":"\\bar{E}_{i\\tau}","meaning":"Upper energy-storage limit for prosumer i at interval \\tau.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined upper energy storage limit"},"unit":"per-contract units","indices":["i","\\tau"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E22"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::theta::theta-E-upper"},{"parameter_id":"theta-PD-t","symbol":"P^{D}_{t}","meaning":"Predicted pre-flexibility-market retail demand at interval t.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined predicted pre-flexibility retail demand"},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::theta::theta-PD-t"},{"parameter_id":"theta-PD-lower","symbol":"\\underline{P}^{D}_{t}","meaning":"Minimum total distribution-network demand limit at interval t.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined minimum distribution-network demand limit"},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::theta::theta-PD-lower"},{"parameter_id":"theta-PD-upper","symbol":"\\bar{P}^{D}_{t}","meaning":"Maximum total distribution-network demand limit at interval t.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined maximum distribution-network demand limit"},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::theta::theta-PD-upper"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-T001","symbol":"Ω","meaning":"Full set of flexibility trades in the market.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Full set of trades in the market"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e21","v141-b-g02-e23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003-T001"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-T002","symbol":"Δp","meaning":"Minimum price-increment added to a trade side's price for an over-demanded trade; the case study uses Δp = £0.005/kWh.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Minimum price-increment Δp; case-study value £0.005/kWh"},"unit":"£/kWh (case study)","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e20","v141-b-g02-e23","v141-b-g02-e29"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003-T002"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::clearing.local","stage":"L5","subtype":"contract_bundle_selection / feasible_contract_outcome / allocation_stability / pareto_efficiency","projection_status":"composite","target_definition":"Includes different L5 objectives: an entity selects a contract subset according to C_i(X)=argmax_{X'_i⊆X_i}U_i(X'_i); the market outcome X' satisfies that each selected transaction has at most one contract and the entity feasibility conditions; stability requires individual rationality and the absence of a feasible non-empty blocking contract set; there is also a Pareto efficiency requirement that no participant can be improved without harming others.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["contract_bundle_selection: the available contract set yields the selected-contract correspondence C_i through individual utility maximization.","feasible_contract_outcome: feasibility of the selected contract set X' and of at most one contract per transaction.","allocation_stability: a result predicate of individual rationality and the absence of a blocking contract set.","pareto_efficiency: no improvement exists that does not harm other participants."],"variation_fields":["Day-ahead multi-period contract selection by the DSO, aggregators and prosumers.","The adjusted power of the prosumer is P^f_it−|Y_it|+|Z_it|, and is simultaneously subject to upper and lower bounds on power and cumulative stored energy.","The adjusted total demand of the DSO is P^D_t−|Y_dt|+|Z_dt|; ultimately satisfying the upper and lower bounds is conditional on sufficient resource flexibility.","An aggregator without its own energy activity satisfies |Y_jt|=|Z_jt| in each time period; the extension with its own flexible resources replaces this balance condition with power and energy constraints.","Completely substitutable preferences support negotiation yielding stable outcomes; Pareto efficiency here is an outcome requirement.","When the one-step improvement property is satisfied, first select the minimum number of contracts required to satisfy the constraints, then repeatedly select the maximum utility improvement until there is no gain."],"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C007","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C008","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C009","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C010","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C011","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001"],"paper_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.local","template_code":"BCT"}],"source_variants":[],"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/146"});
